 ## Overview
-Tensor-to-Metric Transformation is a global visualization method for symmetric second-order tensor fields (e.g., stress, strain, or symmetric gradient) that interprets the tensor as a distortion of a flat metric [S1][S2, book][S3]. It transforms the tensor field into a metric tensor field, which is then visualized using a texture resembling fabric, where eigenvalues control texture density (compression vs. expansion) and eigenvectors control alignment [S1][S2, book]. Unlike [Domain Colouring](1833-domain-colouring.md) or [Glyph-based Vector/Tensor Visualization](10535-glyph-based-vector-tensor-visualization.md), which often suffer from clutter or local view limitations, this method provides a continuous, global representation that explicitly distinguishes between positive (expansion) and negative (compression) eigenvalues by mapping them to different metric values [S2, book][S3].
+Tensor-to-Metric Transformation is a global visualization method for symmetric second-order tensor fields (e.g., stress, strain, or symmetric gradient) that interprets the tensor as a distortion of a flat metric [S1][S2, book][S3]. It transforms the tensor field into a metric tensor field, which is then visualized using a texture resembling fabric, where eigenvalues control texture density (compression vs. expansion) and eigenvectors control alignment [S1][S2, book]. Unlike traditional icon and hyperstreamline methods that focus on eigendirections and neglect eigenvalues, and unlike probe-based methods that reduce the tensor to a vector via inner product, this method explicitly integrates the sign of the eigenvalues to distinguish compression from expansion [S2, book][S3]. Unlike isometric embedding which is restricted to 2D and local, this method provides a global representation using texture deformation [S2, book][S3].
 
